WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.784 --> 00:00:01.617
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.617 --> 00:00:03.014
Sal here from Khan Academy.

00:00:03.014 --> 00:00:05.124
Welcome to our Homeroom live stream,

00:00:05.124 --> 00:00:07.414
very excited about the
conversation we're gonna have

00:00:07.414 --> 00:00:09.054
with Linda Darling-Hammond.

00:00:09.054 --> 00:00:10.214
Before we jump into that,

00:00:10.214 --> 00:00:11.904
I'll give my standard announcements.

00:00:11.904 --> 00:00:13.914
First, a reminder that Khan Academy

00:00:13.914 --> 00:00:16.144
is a not-for-profit organization.

00:00:16.144 --> 00:00:18.464
We can only exist and do the work we do

00:00:18.464 --> 00:00:21.134
because of philanthropic donations
from folks like yourself.

00:00:21.134 --> 00:00:23.444
So if you believe in a
free world class education

00:00:23.444 --> 00:00:25.494
for anyone anywhere, which I hope you do,

00:00:25.494 --> 00:00:28.464
please think about going
to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:28.464 --> 00:00:30.394
I also wanna give a special shout out

00:00:30.394 --> 00:00:32.624
to several organizations that stepped up,

00:00:32.624 --> 00:00:34.194
especially as COVID was hitting.

00:00:34.194 --> 00:00:36.504
We were already running
at a deficit before COVID

00:00:36.504 --> 00:00:37.604
and as you can imagine,

00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:39.384
our server costs have
gone up dramatically.

00:00:39.384 --> 00:00:41.864
We're trying to accelerate
a whole series of programs

00:00:41.864 --> 00:00:45.134
and various efforts and
so our deficits only grew,

00:00:45.134 --> 00:00:47.224
and to help us close some of that,

00:00:47.224 --> 00:00:50.854
a special thanks Bank of
America, Google.org, AT&amp;T,

00:00:50.854 --> 00:00:54.594
Fastly, Novartis, and actually
the Amgen foundation as well,

00:00:54.594 --> 00:00:58.294
and many other supporters,
foundations, corporations

00:00:58.294 --> 00:00:59.954
that have helped Khan
Academy get to the point

00:00:59.954 --> 00:01:03.794
that we could be of
service during this crisis.

00:01:03.794 --> 00:01:05.784
Now with that, I'm really excited

00:01:05.784 --> 00:01:08.644
to introduce our guest
Linda Darling-Hammond,

00:01:08.644 --> 00:01:11.354
who I would describe on many dimensions.

00:01:11.354 --> 00:01:14.997
Stanford professor, hopefully
she considers me a friend,

00:01:16.204 --> 00:01:18.554
president of the California
State Board of Education

00:01:18.554 --> 00:01:23.104
and the founding president of
the Learning Policy Institute.

00:01:23.104 --> 00:01:25.814
So Linda, thanks for joining us.

00:01:25.814 --> 00:01:26.877
- Happy to be here.

00:01:28.264 --> 00:01:29.394
- So maybe our first question.

00:01:29.394 --> 00:01:30.734
I encourage anyone who's watching,

00:01:30.734 --> 00:01:31.834
if you have questions for Linda

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:34.834
who's an expert really in all
things, education and beyond,

00:01:34.834 --> 00:01:36.624
put your questions on the message board

00:01:36.624 --> 00:01:37.585
on Facebook and YouTube

00:01:37.585 --> 00:01:40.044
and we have team members
who will help surface it

00:01:40.044 --> 00:01:40.974
to Linda and myself.

00:01:40.974 --> 00:01:42.524
But maybe our starting point is

00:01:43.384 --> 00:01:46.524
given the crisis that we're in around

00:01:46.524 --> 00:01:48.374
the COVID, distance learning,

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:50.784
where do you think we are in this journey?

00:01:50.784 --> 00:01:51.617
How are you thinking about it?

00:01:51.617 --> 00:01:54.424
What do you think are the most
pressing issues right now?

00:01:55.884 --> 00:01:57.004
- Well, there's several issues.

00:01:57.004 --> 00:01:58.954
I mean, one is obviously

00:01:58.954 --> 00:02:00.504
because we are in distance learning,

00:02:00.504 --> 00:02:03.314
doing that as well as
we can possibly do it.

00:02:03.314 --> 00:02:05.914
And that means, of course,
closing the digital divide,

00:02:05.914 --> 00:02:07.934
we're working hard on that in California.

00:02:07.934 --> 00:02:10.514
It means being sure that teachers

00:02:10.514 --> 00:02:13.774
have the training to use the
tools and materials well,

00:02:13.774 --> 00:02:16.814
synchronous and asynchronous
instruction in the right ways.

00:02:16.814 --> 00:02:20.264
It also means social and
emotional wraparound supports.

00:02:20.264 --> 00:02:23.334
Kids are going through a lot of trauma

00:02:23.334 --> 00:02:25.164
in many, many communities

00:02:25.164 --> 00:02:28.764
and the experience that kids
are having is very disparate.

00:02:28.764 --> 00:02:32.054
Some kids are home with
parents who can work at home.

00:02:32.054 --> 00:02:33.304
Others are having to be home

00:02:33.304 --> 00:02:35.334
while their parents are at work.

00:02:35.334 --> 00:02:37.324
Some have parents unemployed,

00:02:37.324 --> 00:02:40.384
there are health crises in
a number of communities.

00:02:40.384 --> 00:02:42.404
So whatever we're doing
on distance learning

00:02:42.404 --> 00:02:46.804
has to have a social and
emotional support component to it,

00:02:46.804 --> 00:02:48.554
figuring out what kids need,

00:02:48.554 --> 00:02:52.114
making sure that the school
resources to support those needs

00:02:52.114 --> 00:02:56.694
are also available, counseling
and other kinds of resources,

00:02:56.694 --> 00:03:00.184
and then getting solid
distance learning mounted.

00:03:00.184 --> 00:03:03.444
And it's happening in
some very creative ways

00:03:03.444 --> 00:03:06.024
in some districts across the country

00:03:06.024 --> 00:03:07.424
and certainly in California.

00:03:08.724 --> 00:03:11.274
- And what would you say,
given that I completely agree,

00:03:11.274 --> 00:03:13.784
because there's been so
much conversation about

00:03:13.784 --> 00:03:16.584
do schools open or do they go
to distance learning or not

00:03:16.584 --> 00:03:18.814
that it feels like there's
been a little bit of a gap

00:03:18.814 --> 00:03:21.694
and clear guidance on what good

00:03:21.694 --> 00:03:23.954
or even adequate distance
learning looks like,

00:03:23.954 --> 00:03:27.644
do you have examples of
places that are doing it well

00:03:27.644 --> 00:03:29.504
that could be emulated by other places

00:03:29.504 --> 00:03:31.044
or just general guidelines?

00:03:31.044 --> 00:03:32.884
We've actually been
trying to do it on our own

00:03:32.884 --> 00:03:35.194
so it's good for us to sync up on this.

00:03:35.194 --> 00:03:37.354
- Yeah, well the California
Department of Education

00:03:37.354 --> 00:03:40.894
just released some guidance
this week on distance learning

00:03:40.894 --> 00:03:44.314
that does sort of summarize
what some of the research shows,

00:03:44.314 --> 00:03:46.434
matters in distance learning.

00:03:46.434 --> 00:03:48.644
And then there are
districts across the state

00:03:48.644 --> 00:03:50.834
that are doing really exciting work.

00:03:50.834 --> 00:03:53.260
Among the things that matter are

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.944
having everyone sort of
on a common platform,

00:03:56.944 --> 00:04:00.764
training parents as well as
students as well as teachers

00:04:00.764 --> 00:04:04.904
in how to access the materials and so on,

00:04:04.904 --> 00:04:09.464
having interactive
instruction synchronously,

00:04:09.464 --> 00:04:12.074
it's not like just the stage on the stage.

00:04:12.074 --> 00:04:17.074
It's being sure that the things
that are being communicated

00:04:17.954 --> 00:04:21.394
can be discussed among
kids in breakout rooms

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:23.134
and in chats and so on

00:04:23.134 --> 00:04:25.274
being sure that interactive
element is there,

00:04:25.274 --> 00:04:29.344
having good materials for
asynchronous instruction.

00:04:29.344 --> 00:04:32.064
And you're of course familiar
with Khan Academy materials

00:04:32.064 --> 00:04:34.614
that a lot of districts are
using and there are others

00:04:34.614 --> 00:04:37.874
across content areas that
when those themselves

00:04:37.874 --> 00:04:39.584
are well scaffolded,

00:04:39.584 --> 00:04:42.661
where we've done the right
kind of assessment to tell kids

00:04:42.661 --> 00:04:47.334
where to tune in on the things
that they need to work on

00:04:47.334 --> 00:04:50.394
and then bring it back
in line with the teacher

00:04:50.394 --> 00:04:52.764
and the peers for further discussion.

00:04:52.764 --> 00:04:56.444
You can get very substantial
gains in learning

00:04:56.444 --> 00:04:58.884
if you've got all of
those components in place.

00:04:58.884 --> 00:05:01.404
And that's what we're trying
to make sure is happening.

00:05:01.404 --> 00:05:04.224
Long Beach is a place in California

00:05:04.224 --> 00:05:07.074
that's doing really
interesting work around this,

00:05:07.074 --> 00:05:09.064
San Diego many others.

00:05:09.064 --> 00:05:11.734
I will say in Long Beach
one of the things I love

00:05:11.734 --> 00:05:13.474
and they are actually,

00:05:13.474 --> 00:05:15.924
it happens using some kind of materials

00:05:15.924 --> 00:05:19.584
but they're having teachers teach in ways,

00:05:19.584 --> 00:05:24.334
and this is similar to your
approach to reaching everyone,

00:05:24.334 --> 00:05:25.234
the great teachers

00:05:25.234 --> 00:05:27.074
who are really good on certain topic areas

00:05:27.074 --> 00:05:31.664
are giving courses and
lessons anyone can tune into.

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:35.154
So sometimes thousands of
kids tune in to teachers

00:05:35.154 --> 00:05:37.254
who are teaching particular things,

00:05:37.254 --> 00:05:40.424
other teachers can tune in
and learn the strategies

00:05:40.424 --> 00:05:42.154
that that teacher is using

00:05:42.154 --> 00:05:44.544
and then they can use the materials

00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:48.244
that are available for practice
and for the next stages.

00:05:48.244 --> 00:05:50.784
So there are ways that the technology

00:05:50.784 --> 00:05:55.664
can really leverage great
teaching and materials

00:05:55.664 --> 00:05:59.231
that we couldn't do when we
were just in single classrooms,

00:05:59.231 --> 00:06:02.844
that we need to learn how to
take advantage of right now.

00:06:02.844 --> 00:06:05.291
- That's super valuable (mumbles)

00:06:05.291 --> 00:06:06.124
this is a school district

00:06:06.124 --> 00:06:07.484
that we've been working very closely with

00:06:07.484 --> 00:06:10.084
at Khan Academy for many
years and they have a recently

00:06:10.084 --> 00:06:11.714
retiring superintendent,
Chris Steinhauser,

00:06:11.714 --> 00:06:14.034
but the whole team there
they've really been always

00:06:14.034 --> 00:06:15.534
one of the most innovative
school districts.

00:06:15.534 --> 00:06:16.750
And actually that project,

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:18.204
that's actually a little skunkworks

00:06:18.204 --> 00:06:19.974
that are at least aspects
of what you described

00:06:19.974 --> 00:06:21.907
is that I've been working on with them.

00:06:25.494 --> 00:06:27.104
Well maybe there's multiple projects,

00:06:27.104 --> 00:06:28.374
but yeah we're doing that exactly

00:06:28.374 --> 00:06:31.304
so that some of their top
teachers in the district

00:06:31.304 --> 00:06:34.084
can set up group sessions
that any kid in the district

00:06:34.084 --> 00:06:34.917
can attend.

00:06:34.917 --> 00:06:37.694
So you're no longer tied
to your specific zip code

00:06:37.694 --> 00:06:39.654
or neighborhood anymore.

00:06:39.654 --> 00:06:43.014
- And they've had some where
2000 kids have shown up.

00:06:43.014 --> 00:06:47.044
'Cause teachers can be
famous just like movie stars

00:06:47.044 --> 00:06:49.784
for the amazing work that they do.

00:06:49.784 --> 00:06:52.754
And people can get that experience.

00:06:52.754 --> 00:06:56.874
I think this is all about
inventing and sharing.

00:06:56.874 --> 00:06:59.237
That's what this moment
in history is about,

00:07:00.754 --> 00:07:03.614
human beings invent, that's what we do,

00:07:03.614 --> 00:07:06.414
we inquire and we invent
and then we've got to share

00:07:06.414 --> 00:07:07.907
what we're learning.

00:07:09.154 --> 00:07:10.856
- And just to double click on that,

00:07:10.856 --> 00:07:13.814
everything you said makes
all the sense in the world

00:07:13.814 --> 00:07:14.764
and it's very much aligned

00:07:14.764 --> 00:07:17.224
with a lot of what we've
been talking about as well.

00:07:17.224 --> 00:07:19.674
But if I'm a teacher I'm used to doing,

00:07:19.674 --> 00:07:22.514
let's say five days a week, an hour a day,

00:07:22.514 --> 00:07:26.604
maybe I spend some time grading
papers, et cetera at home

00:07:26.604 --> 00:07:28.084
and lesson planning,

00:07:28.084 --> 00:07:30.504
how does it I think the guidance around

00:07:30.504 --> 00:07:33.604
making the video conferencing
sessions more interactive,

00:07:33.604 --> 00:07:35.964
but does it become

00:07:35.964 --> 00:07:38.784
five, one hour video
conference sessions a week,

00:07:38.784 --> 00:07:40.274
what would you recommend that teacher do?

00:07:40.274 --> 00:07:41.864
Do they do fewer shorter ones?

00:07:41.864 --> 00:07:43.684
Should they do smaller groups?

00:07:43.684 --> 00:07:45.857
Or it's really, should
they just experiment

00:07:45.857 --> 00:07:47.904
and see what works?

00:07:47.904 --> 00:07:51.024
- Well, it does depend on
the age level of the kids

00:07:51.024 --> 00:07:53.044
as well as what the kids have had before

00:07:53.044 --> 00:07:55.254
so it's a really challenging thing

00:07:55.254 --> 00:07:57.964
and I just wanna say this, if
you're teaching little kids

00:07:57.964 --> 00:08:00.684
and you're trying to do it
online, that's very challenging

00:08:00.684 --> 00:08:02.654
'cause they're squirrely and so on,

00:08:02.654 --> 00:08:06.034
but that's where you need
a lot more interactive

00:08:06.034 --> 00:08:09.257
kind of materials, very short sessions,

00:08:10.464 --> 00:08:13.814
interspersed with some physical activity,

00:08:13.814 --> 00:08:17.824
you're doing some PE in
the middle of the time

00:08:17.824 --> 00:08:18.824
that you're working,

00:08:18.824 --> 00:08:23.174
you may have low budget
art, kinds of things

00:08:23.174 --> 00:08:24.764
that everybody can engage in

00:08:25.854 --> 00:08:28.394
if you're working with
smaller kids, for example,

00:08:28.394 --> 00:08:29.674
in the middle of instruction

00:08:29.674 --> 00:08:31.464
where you're also reading to them.

00:08:31.464 --> 00:08:36.464
But generally speaking modest
amounts of direct instruction,

00:08:37.644 --> 00:08:40.534
short amounts of that
with lots of opportunities

00:08:40.534 --> 00:08:41.567
for interactions.

00:08:42.524 --> 00:08:46.234
The Zoom breakout room
is a wonderful thing

00:08:46.234 --> 00:08:47.944
and when kids get old enough

00:08:47.944 --> 00:08:50.224
and have been taught
how to work in groups,

00:08:50.224 --> 00:08:53.694
you can pose some information,
you can pose the problem,

00:08:53.694 --> 00:08:55.274
you can pose the question,

00:08:55.274 --> 00:08:58.094
send them off to work on it
and talk about it for a while,

00:08:58.094 --> 00:09:01.394
have them come back and
share their solutions

00:09:01.394 --> 00:09:05.584
if it's in a math or science
context or their ideas,

00:09:05.584 --> 00:09:08.534
their decisions in a debate context,

00:09:08.534 --> 00:09:11.444
you can have them engaged
in debates and projects.

00:09:11.444 --> 00:09:15.774
Some of the asynchronous time
can also be done collectively

00:09:15.774 --> 00:09:20.094
because a lot of education is
doing things both individually

00:09:20.094 --> 00:09:22.704
and then talking about it with others

00:09:22.704 --> 00:09:25.544
and teaching kids how to
manage their own learning.

00:09:25.544 --> 00:09:28.074
This is an opportunity for us to help kids

00:09:28.074 --> 00:09:31.714
take agency in learning
and to really teach them

00:09:31.714 --> 00:09:35.494
how to set up their own sort
of learning management systems,

00:09:35.494 --> 00:09:37.294
how to use the materials

00:09:37.294 --> 00:09:40.704
that they're gonna use
asynchronously well,

00:09:40.704 --> 00:09:42.344
so that they're learning how to learn

00:09:42.344 --> 00:09:47.094
because that's the critical
skill for 21st century society,

00:09:47.094 --> 00:09:51.104
is being a self managing learner.

00:09:51.104 --> 00:09:53.564
This is the time to get away
from transmission teaching

00:09:53.564 --> 00:09:55.894
which is I'm talking and
you're just writing it down

00:09:55.894 --> 00:09:57.664
and spitting it back on a test

00:09:57.664 --> 00:10:02.234
towards teaching kids to
be increasingly engaged

00:10:02.234 --> 00:10:03.754
in self-managing in their learning.

00:10:03.754 --> 00:10:05.474
And we've seen that the schools

00:10:05.474 --> 00:10:07.854
that are project based schools,

00:10:07.854 --> 00:10:10.634
where kids are engaged in
doing projects individually

00:10:10.634 --> 00:10:15.364
and collectively have
had hardly missed a beat

00:10:15.364 --> 00:10:18.374
in the switch to online

00:10:18.374 --> 00:10:21.094
because the kids were
already learning how to plan

00:10:21.094 --> 00:10:23.164
and manage parts of their learning.

00:10:23.164 --> 00:10:24.944
They were devoted to their projects

00:10:24.944 --> 00:10:26.144
'cause they cared about them,

00:10:26.144 --> 00:10:28.664
they were preparing their presentations.

00:10:28.664 --> 00:10:29.657
They could present them.

00:10:29.657 --> 00:10:31.254
Part of the homework sometimes

00:10:31.254 --> 00:10:33.314
is go present it to your grandparents,

00:10:33.314 --> 00:10:35.694
I love that kind of homework
'cause I'm a grandmother

00:10:35.694 --> 00:10:40.634
and you want your kids to
have audiences for their work.

00:10:40.634 --> 00:10:42.684
And so they present in a variety of ways,

00:10:42.684 --> 00:10:44.544
but then the formal presentations

00:10:44.544 --> 00:10:49.274
at the end of the year
often, to a broader audience.

00:10:49.274 --> 00:10:50.774
In one case in Oakland,

00:10:50.774 --> 00:10:53.884
the kids have been working on
a safety plan for their school

00:10:53.884 --> 00:10:57.214
because it was a very
dangerous school to get to.

00:10:57.214 --> 00:10:59.774
Their last end of the year presentation

00:10:59.774 --> 00:11:01.734
was to the city council

00:11:01.734 --> 00:11:04.934
who actually took up some new policies

00:11:04.934 --> 00:11:07.614
to make it safer for
kids to get to school.

00:11:07.614 --> 00:11:09.197
So when they do go live,

00:11:10.114 --> 00:11:13.094
they'll have a new path to get there.

00:11:13.094 --> 00:11:14.554
So I think that's part of it,

00:11:14.554 --> 00:11:19.024
keeping kids engaged in ways
that they really care about

00:11:19.024 --> 00:11:23.064
and helping them learn
to be more and more able

00:11:23.064 --> 00:11:24.657
to manage their own learning.

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:26.791
- And what's interesting about everything

00:11:26.791 --> 00:11:29.754
you just mentioned is those
sound like best practices,

00:11:29.754 --> 00:11:31.534
even if we were doing in-person learning,

00:11:31.534 --> 00:11:33.684
make the students interact
as much as possible,

00:11:33.684 --> 00:11:36.954
have them take agency, have
them apply what they're doing,

00:11:36.954 --> 00:11:39.754
maybe have an audience to see
the artifacts of the work.

00:11:41.604 --> 00:11:44.194
What are your biggest fears

00:11:44.194 --> 00:11:46.264
about the situation
that we're in right now?

00:11:46.264 --> 00:11:47.644
And do you see some silver linings?

00:11:47.644 --> 00:11:49.124
Maybe one of them is that

00:11:49.124 --> 00:11:51.474
some of these practices
become more mainstream.

00:11:52.555 --> 00:11:53.664
- Well, of course there are fears.

00:11:53.664 --> 00:11:56.744
I mean, we have a huge
equity gap in this country.

00:11:56.744 --> 00:12:00.264
I mean, we fund schools inequitably,

00:12:00.264 --> 00:12:02.444
we have a huge income gap,

00:12:02.444 --> 00:12:05.664
the communities that are
being affected by COVID

00:12:05.664 --> 00:12:07.464
in the black and brown communities

00:12:07.464 --> 00:12:11.684
that have been hit employment
wise and health wise

00:12:11.684 --> 00:12:16.574
and in a variety of ways,
those are huge huge problems

00:12:16.574 --> 00:12:19.224
that we confront all the time

00:12:19.224 --> 00:12:21.694
and they are exacerbated right now.

00:12:21.694 --> 00:12:25.904
So the other piece of what's
needed, and we saw this too.

00:12:25.904 --> 00:12:28.114
The schools that were community schools

00:12:28.114 --> 00:12:30.114
that had social workers and

00:12:30.114 --> 00:12:33.044
wrap around health and mental
health services already

00:12:33.044 --> 00:12:34.094
engaged in school

00:12:34.094 --> 00:12:36.844
were able to meet the
needs of the families

00:12:36.844 --> 00:12:37.704
in a much better way.

00:12:37.704 --> 00:12:38.774
We got to do that,

00:12:38.774 --> 00:12:43.774
a lot of districts are doing
one on one even distanced,

00:12:44.064 --> 00:12:49.064
socially distanced home
visits, where that's needed

00:12:49.684 --> 00:12:53.404
as well as one-on-one on
the phone and on the Zoom.

00:12:53.404 --> 00:12:56.034
And in other ways to connect with families

00:12:56.034 --> 00:12:58.324
and ensure that kids are
getting what they need.

00:12:58.324 --> 00:13:00.174
We're gonna need distance learning hubs

00:13:00.174 --> 00:13:03.494
and San Francisco's putting
up distance learning hubs

00:13:03.494 --> 00:13:06.504
for 6,000 kids where
they can come to a place

00:13:06.504 --> 00:13:09.984
where they can get food
and they can get equipment

00:13:09.984 --> 00:13:13.554
and they can be online and
they can also have counselors

00:13:13.554 --> 00:13:15.814
and social workers and
others to be available

00:13:15.814 --> 00:13:19.684
to be sure that their whole
child needs are being met.

00:13:19.684 --> 00:13:22.234
So that's the biggest
fear is that we won't

00:13:22.234 --> 00:13:24.504
step up and be sure that kids are

00:13:25.354 --> 00:13:28.484
surrounded with all of
the care that they need.

00:13:28.484 --> 00:13:31.704
But it is happening and
I'm just very inspired

00:13:31.704 --> 00:13:36.284
by the way that educators
and others are stepping up.

00:13:36.284 --> 00:13:40.204
The silver linings are very interesting

00:13:40.204 --> 00:13:44.054
because if you go back to
the beginning of March,

00:13:44.054 --> 00:13:47.884
we had this digital divide
where about 30% of kids,

00:13:47.884 --> 00:13:51.237
20 to 30% either didn't have connectivity

00:13:51.237 --> 00:13:54.394
or devices that they could use at home.

00:13:54.394 --> 00:13:55.344
In California,

00:13:55.344 --> 00:13:57.904
we've probably closed
that divide by about half.

00:13:57.904 --> 00:14:02.024
We have $5 billion going
out to schools right now

00:14:02.024 --> 00:14:03.974
as part of the federal money,

00:14:03.974 --> 00:14:06.924
as well as a lot of corporations of course

00:14:06.924 --> 00:14:10.964
stepped up a lot of districts,
went into their reserves

00:14:10.964 --> 00:14:12.834
and bought computers,

00:14:12.834 --> 00:14:15.274
a lot of companies are
figuring out how to get

00:14:16.294 --> 00:14:19.746
digital access in much
more reasonable ways.

00:14:19.746 --> 00:14:23.944
We should and could have
closed the digital divide

00:14:24.784 --> 00:14:27.344
by the end this year.

00:14:27.344 --> 00:14:31.184
Well that wasn't gonna happen
unless we had that necessity.

00:14:31.184 --> 00:14:33.804
We will have teachers and students

00:14:33.804 --> 00:14:36.814
and other staff technologically proficient

00:14:36.814 --> 00:14:39.174
in ways that would not have happened

00:14:39.174 --> 00:14:40.991
unless we had to do that.

00:14:40.991 --> 00:14:43.924
And if you think about
our economy in California

00:14:43.924 --> 00:14:45.844
and I don't mean to leave
out the rest of the country,

00:14:45.844 --> 00:14:48.114
this is true elsewhere also,

00:14:48.114 --> 00:14:51.344
the technological
underpinnings of the economy

00:14:51.344 --> 00:14:54.544
are growing at an exponential rate.

00:14:54.544 --> 00:14:56.664
So the fact that we're gonna have

00:14:56.664 --> 00:15:01.194
a technologically proficient populace

00:15:01.194 --> 00:15:05.864
that cuts across lines of class and income

00:15:05.864 --> 00:15:10.774
in ways that we didn't
previously is a huge gain,

00:15:10.774 --> 00:15:12.274
we need to take advantage of it.

00:15:12.274 --> 00:15:14.744
And people are learning
to teach in new ways

00:15:14.744 --> 00:15:19.094
that we need to capture, understand, share

00:15:19.094 --> 00:15:20.477
and take advantage of.

00:15:21.644 --> 00:15:23.384
- That makes a ton of sense.

00:15:23.384 --> 00:15:26.974
And we're getting a ton of
questions here on social media.

00:15:26.974 --> 00:15:28.604
One question, and this is a question

00:15:28.604 --> 00:15:30.124
that I have heard a little bit too

00:15:30.124 --> 00:15:32.347
from Facebook Zahid Zia is asking,

00:15:33.254 --> 00:15:34.694
it sounds like Zahid is a teacher,

00:15:34.694 --> 00:15:37.254
I'm struggling on how to perform
quick formative assessment

00:15:37.254 --> 00:15:38.087
in a virtual classroom.

00:15:38.087 --> 00:15:40.374
For those of you all who
aren't used to the language,

00:15:40.374 --> 00:15:42.924
formative assessment is
assessment as kids are learning

00:15:42.924 --> 00:15:44.414
so that a teacher can understand

00:15:44.414 --> 00:15:45.674
how well they've learned the material

00:15:45.674 --> 00:15:47.134
and where they might need extra help.

00:15:47.134 --> 00:15:49.184
And I'll extend that question,

00:15:49.184 --> 00:15:50.594
a lot of the school districts last year

00:15:50.594 --> 00:15:53.504
had to just go to pass
fail, cause they're like,

00:15:53.504 --> 00:15:55.394
not everyone has equal access.

00:15:55.394 --> 00:15:57.684
Even now I'm hearing a lot
of teachers are struggling

00:15:57.684 --> 00:15:58.684
with Zahid's question

00:15:58.684 --> 00:16:00.774
or how do I do assessments
in a world where

00:16:00.774 --> 00:16:03.184
I can't proctor them?

00:16:03.184 --> 00:16:05.044
And this is all happening at the same time

00:16:05.044 --> 00:16:07.324
that things like standardized assessments

00:16:07.324 --> 00:16:09.084
are getting difficult to administer.

00:16:09.084 --> 00:16:11.374
So is there kind of an information gap

00:16:11.374 --> 00:16:13.687
that's developing because of this crisis?

00:16:15.634 --> 00:16:17.834
- You mean information
about how kids are doing...

00:16:17.834 --> 00:16:19.004
- About how kids are doing

00:16:19.004 --> 00:16:21.296
and what they've learned and have learned.

00:16:21.296 --> 00:16:25.204
- There may be, I do think
that we can help teachers

00:16:25.204 --> 00:16:28.354
with tools for formative
and diagnostic assessment.

00:16:28.354 --> 00:16:29.924
One of the things we did in California,

00:16:29.924 --> 00:16:32.714
it's on the CD website
is a guidance around

00:16:32.714 --> 00:16:35.354
using diagnostic and
formative assessments.

00:16:35.354 --> 00:16:37.604
And we've approved in the state

00:16:37.604 --> 00:16:40.189
about probably a dozen different

00:16:40.189 --> 00:16:44.764
formative and diagnostic
assessment tools and programs

00:16:44.764 --> 00:16:45.904
that people can access.

00:16:45.904 --> 00:16:48.064
Some districts are using them regularly

00:16:48.064 --> 00:16:50.824
so that you can see where
kids are on a regular basis.

00:16:50.824 --> 00:16:54.254
A lot of them are very short assessments.

00:16:54.254 --> 00:16:57.084
The smarter balanced assessment system

00:16:57.084 --> 00:17:00.964
has all kinds of formative
and interim assessment tools,

00:17:00.964 --> 00:17:03.134
they are freely available
to every teacher,

00:17:03.134 --> 00:17:04.584
public and private schools

00:17:06.064 --> 00:17:07.771
and that you can burrow in on a topic area

00:17:07.771 --> 00:17:11.964
to see where kids are kind of in a domain

00:17:11.964 --> 00:17:13.624
as well as more broadly.

00:17:13.624 --> 00:17:16.654
And so we want teachers
to get familiar with

00:17:16.654 --> 00:17:18.144
how to use these tools.

00:17:18.144 --> 00:17:20.174
They don't require proctoring,

00:17:20.174 --> 00:17:24.174
they don't require some
special testing context.

00:17:24.174 --> 00:17:26.564
They just require the time and the tools

00:17:26.564 --> 00:17:27.654
right there for kids

00:17:27.654 --> 00:17:30.974
and then you can guide your
instruction accordingly.

00:17:30.974 --> 00:17:34.484
And they usually scale across the entire K

00:17:34.484 --> 00:17:39.374
through eight or 12 scale of progress.

00:17:39.374 --> 00:17:41.924
And then you can figure out
what materials you need to use.

00:17:41.924 --> 00:17:42.804
The other thing, of course,

00:17:42.804 --> 00:17:46.594
our formative assessments that
teachers would do themselves.

00:17:46.594 --> 00:17:48.934
And it's a little harder, perhaps,

00:17:48.934 --> 00:17:51.434
when you've got a video screen of kids

00:17:51.434 --> 00:17:55.344
to check for understanding,
but you can use the chat box,

00:17:55.344 --> 00:17:58.194
you can use exit tickets out the door

00:17:58.194 --> 00:18:01.904
at the end of the class and
say answer this question,

00:18:01.904 --> 00:18:04.614
tell me what you understood
or what you didn't understand

00:18:04.614 --> 00:18:09.574
and begin to get those kinds
of tools directly in the mix

00:18:09.574 --> 00:18:13.657
for the way in which the
learning takes place.

00:18:14.844 --> 00:18:18.114
- And then how would you
handle assessment in terms of

00:18:18.114 --> 00:18:19.614
even giving students grades

00:18:19.614 --> 00:18:21.444
for maybe the K through eight crowd,

00:18:21.444 --> 00:18:22.584
that's not as important.

00:18:22.584 --> 00:18:24.054
The grades are a little
bit lower at stakes,

00:18:24.054 --> 00:18:26.234
but obviously as you go into high school,

00:18:26.234 --> 00:18:28.044
folks really start caring about grades,

00:18:28.044 --> 00:18:29.864
especially as it shows
up on their transcripts,

00:18:29.864 --> 00:18:33.149
it's being used for things
like college admissions,

00:18:33.149 --> 00:18:35.524
how do should a teacher think about it,

00:18:35.524 --> 00:18:36.584
authenticating students?

00:18:36.584 --> 00:18:38.344
Should they just go to a real honor code?

00:18:38.344 --> 00:18:40.729
Are there ways to ensure that

00:18:40.729 --> 00:18:43.024
there isn't some shady behavior going on

00:18:43.024 --> 00:18:45.624
while other people taking
the test or getting help,

00:18:45.624 --> 00:18:46.474
things like that?

00:18:48.004 --> 00:18:52.734
- Well, I do think that
we need to be thinking

00:18:52.734 --> 00:18:56.204
in honor code terms in general
in the society and in life.

00:18:56.204 --> 00:18:59.554
And one of my kids went to
college based on the fact that

00:18:59.554 --> 00:19:00.564
they had an honor code,

00:19:00.564 --> 00:19:03.684
because they wanted to
be really in a place that

00:19:03.684 --> 00:19:07.594
was ethically organized around that idea.

00:19:07.594 --> 00:19:09.594
The more we use authentic assessments,

00:19:09.594 --> 00:19:13.634
the more kids have to sort of
produce and defend and explain

00:19:13.634 --> 00:19:15.724
what they've done and show it,

00:19:15.724 --> 00:19:17.634
the less you have to worry about cheating.

00:19:17.634 --> 00:19:21.157
It's kind of like the
driver's test in the DMV.

00:19:22.374 --> 00:19:24.544
You might be able to
figure out how to cheat

00:19:24.544 --> 00:19:28.104
on the multiple choice test,
but you got to get in that car

00:19:28.104 --> 00:19:29.874
and you've got to drive with the driver

00:19:29.874 --> 00:19:32.414
and show that you can do

00:19:32.414 --> 00:19:34.274
all of the things on the driver's test.

00:19:34.274 --> 00:19:36.404
Now in New York, they
have a higher standard

00:19:36.404 --> 00:19:38.014
because you have to parallel park

00:19:38.014 --> 00:19:39.697
as well as everything else.

00:19:40.814 --> 00:19:42.234
But it is authentic

00:19:42.234 --> 00:19:45.214
and I think the more we work
with authentic assessments

00:19:45.214 --> 00:19:47.674
of learning that kids present and defend,

00:19:47.674 --> 00:19:50.474
the more you can see that
they've really learned it.

00:19:50.474 --> 00:19:53.214
And I think that's the
direction we should be going.

00:19:53.214 --> 00:19:56.824
It's the direction that the
advanced placement assessments,

00:19:56.824 --> 00:19:58.054
the international baccalaureate

00:19:58.054 --> 00:20:02.054
and others have been
going in for some years.

00:20:02.054 --> 00:20:02.944
In terms of grades,

00:20:02.944 --> 00:20:07.194
my view is that we should
always be honoring the learning.

00:20:07.194 --> 00:20:12.144
And the goal is to see what you can get to

00:20:12.144 --> 00:20:14.984
not privileged people

00:20:14.984 --> 00:20:17.434
who've had more experience with something

00:20:17.434 --> 00:20:18.744
who get there faster.

00:20:18.744 --> 00:20:21.354
So I believe in approach

00:20:21.354 --> 00:20:23.824
and this is very well
documented in the research,

00:20:23.824 --> 00:20:28.524
that if you give people the
opportunity to get instruction,

00:20:28.524 --> 00:20:32.914
try an authentic task, get
feedback often using rubrics

00:20:32.914 --> 00:20:35.984
or other tools, try it again, revise it.

00:20:35.984 --> 00:20:40.114
What should count is where
you end up in your learning,

00:20:40.114 --> 00:20:43.424
as you've done this opportunity to engage,

00:20:43.424 --> 00:20:46.854
practice and improve rather
than where you started.

00:20:46.854 --> 00:20:49.274
And that makes it possible for kids

00:20:49.274 --> 00:20:53.644
who started further behind
to show the progress

00:20:53.644 --> 00:20:55.734
because they're getting
the opportunity to iterate.

00:20:55.734 --> 00:20:57.964
If you think about anything we do in life

00:20:57.964 --> 00:21:00.664
that is an actual performance,

00:21:00.664 --> 00:21:04.264
it's through that process of trying,

00:21:04.264 --> 00:21:06.554
getting feedback and trying again,

00:21:06.554 --> 00:21:08.164
and the more we grade like that,

00:21:08.164 --> 00:21:11.834
the more we actually drive
learning upwards for everyone

00:21:11.834 --> 00:21:13.934
and the more we close the achievement gap.

00:21:15.034 --> 00:21:18.054
- I couldn't agree with
you more and you know this,

00:21:18.054 --> 00:21:21.504
but a lot of our resources at
Khan Academy were exactly that

00:21:21.504 --> 00:21:24.214
make sure that kids get as much
practice, as much feedback,

00:21:24.214 --> 00:21:26.024
that if they they're at an 80% state,

00:21:26.024 --> 00:21:28.504
that's not the end of the world,
they can keep working on it

00:21:28.504 --> 00:21:30.324
so that they can eventually
get to where they're going.

00:21:30.324 --> 00:21:32.764
And also I 100% agree with the authentic,

00:21:32.764 --> 00:21:34.364
I've been brainstorming
with some educators

00:21:34.364 --> 00:21:35.384
and we might even make this part of

00:21:35.384 --> 00:21:36.754
that schoolhouse.world project,

00:21:36.754 --> 00:21:39.854
where if a student is
able to take an assessment

00:21:39.854 --> 00:21:42.454
on Khan Academy, say of course
challenger or a unit test,

00:21:42.454 --> 00:21:43.904
and they're taking a screen capture

00:21:43.904 --> 00:21:47.074
while they're talking out
loud to show their reasoning,

00:21:47.074 --> 00:21:48.244
and then they submit that video.

00:21:48.244 --> 00:21:49.854
That's actually very hard to cheat that

00:21:49.854 --> 00:21:52.154
and in a lot of ways, you're
gonna get a lot more texture

00:21:52.154 --> 00:21:53.954
than you would have had even
with a traditional assessment.

00:21:53.954 --> 00:21:57.344
So I think there's going
to be some models that...

00:21:57.344 --> 00:22:00.464
- I love that and you're
gonna get more learning

00:22:00.464 --> 00:22:02.904
because actually when
we explain what we know,

00:22:02.904 --> 00:22:06.594
we're actually solidifying
it in our brains

00:22:06.594 --> 00:22:09.374
in a way that makes it
more deeply understood

00:22:09.374 --> 00:22:12.487
so I think that's the
way we should be going.

00:22:13.534 --> 00:22:14.367
- 100%.

00:22:14.367 --> 00:22:15.924
Well, one question for
you, this is from me,

00:22:15.924 --> 00:22:18.934
I've always wondered, and
I don't know to what degree

00:22:18.934 --> 00:22:21.404
you have jurisdiction over
things like the A to G standards.

00:22:21.404 --> 00:22:22.237
That's probably more than

00:22:22.237 --> 00:22:25.064
the University of California
Regents, but I am curious,

00:22:25.064 --> 00:22:27.854
right now in California they
have the A to G standards.

00:22:27.854 --> 00:22:29.484
Every state has kind of the,

00:22:29.484 --> 00:22:31.534
you need this many years
of foreign language,

00:22:31.534 --> 00:22:33.904
this many years of math,
this many years of English

00:22:33.904 --> 00:22:35.994
in almost every state
and almost every country,

00:22:35.994 --> 00:22:39.084
it's still described very
much in terms of time

00:22:39.084 --> 00:22:40.414
not in terms of outcome.

00:22:40.414 --> 00:22:41.874
And as you just described,

00:22:41.874 --> 00:22:43.624
it's much more valuable to think of things

00:22:43.624 --> 00:22:46.614
in terms of outcome. and some
kids might take a few months,

00:22:46.614 --> 00:22:48.574
some kids might take a
few years to learn it,

00:22:48.574 --> 00:22:51.004
but if they get to that
outcome, that's what matters.

00:22:51.004 --> 00:22:52.074
Is there a movement?

00:22:52.074 --> 00:22:53.104
'Cause I do think these

00:22:53.104 --> 00:22:55.114
A to G type Carnegie unit type things

00:22:55.114 --> 00:22:56.844
are actually one of the main blockers

00:22:56.844 --> 00:22:58.994
to moving to more of
whatever you wanna call it,

00:22:58.994 --> 00:23:01.227
outcome based or competency-based world.

00:23:03.074 --> 00:23:04.854
- Well, there is a
competency based movement.

00:23:04.854 --> 00:23:07.264
There's been one for 30 or 40 years.

00:23:07.264 --> 00:23:09.454
You probably are aware that
there are always people

00:23:09.454 --> 00:23:11.404
talking about this and trying to do it.

00:23:12.354 --> 00:23:14.584
We've kind of been locked in concrete

00:23:14.584 --> 00:23:17.694
around the system of education
that we've had for so long.

00:23:17.694 --> 00:23:20.184
The courses that are in high school

00:23:20.184 --> 00:23:22.050
that most people think about,

00:23:22.050 --> 00:23:25.304
algebra, geometry, and trigonometry

00:23:25.304 --> 00:23:27.454
were taken in that order, alphabetical,

00:23:27.454 --> 00:23:30.364
biology, chemistry, physics,
in that order alphabetical,

00:23:30.364 --> 00:23:32.134
they're in the A through G system,

00:23:32.134 --> 00:23:35.844
those were all developed by
the committee of 10 and 1890.

00:23:35.844 --> 00:23:38.544
It is time for a new approach.

00:23:38.544 --> 00:23:43.074
I'm actually just now joining
an international committee

00:23:43.074 --> 00:23:45.244
that OACD is forming

00:23:45.244 --> 00:23:47.704
because I've been talking with
folks there for a long time

00:23:47.704 --> 00:23:50.494
about we need to have a new committee

00:23:50.494 --> 00:23:53.894
to think about what it really
means to have a curriculum

00:23:53.894 --> 00:23:57.704
for the 21st century
which we're well into now,

00:23:57.704 --> 00:24:00.484
but we haven't caught up
kind of in terms of our,

00:24:00.484 --> 00:24:03.244
and this moment of disruption
in the education system

00:24:03.244 --> 00:24:06.904
is causing people to
think, what really matters?

00:24:06.904 --> 00:24:08.484
What should we really be doing here?

00:24:08.484 --> 00:24:09.774
How do we do it?

00:24:09.774 --> 00:24:12.521
What's really important in addition to

00:24:12.521 --> 00:24:16.114
certain kinds of content
knowledge for sure,

00:24:16.114 --> 00:24:18.174
is the habits of mind,

00:24:18.174 --> 00:24:21.184
the way in which you can learn to learn,

00:24:21.184 --> 00:24:26.184
more knowledge was created
between 1999 and 2003.

00:24:26.314 --> 00:24:29.954
Then in the entire history
of the world proceeding,

00:24:29.954 --> 00:24:32.587
knowledge is exponentially increasing.

00:24:33.804 --> 00:24:36.384
Technology knowledge is doubling

00:24:36.384 --> 00:24:39.694
probably faster than every
11 months at this point.

00:24:39.694 --> 00:24:43.044
So people need some
understanding of the concepts

00:24:43.044 --> 00:24:45.674
and structures and factual basis,

00:24:45.674 --> 00:24:48.844
but they also need a
huge amount of capacity

00:24:48.844 --> 00:24:50.284
to learn to learn.

00:24:50.284 --> 00:24:53.294
And that's what we should be engendering

00:24:53.294 --> 00:24:55.284
because kids are gonna
be working with knowledge

00:24:55.284 --> 00:24:57.864
that hasn't been discovered yet

00:24:57.864 --> 00:25:00.804
using technologies that
haven't been invented yet

00:25:00.804 --> 00:25:04.074
to solve enormous problems in the world

00:25:04.074 --> 00:25:06.014
that we have not managed to solve.

00:25:06.014 --> 00:25:10.094
And our way of counting
progress in education

00:25:10.094 --> 00:25:13.314
doesn't take these things
into account right now.

00:25:13.314 --> 00:25:16.304
There are places, New
Hampshire is one of them

00:25:16.304 --> 00:25:17.137
across the country

00:25:17.137 --> 00:25:19.914
that's really trying to get
to a competency based system

00:25:19.914 --> 00:25:22.164
and there are others
that are working on it,

00:25:22.164 --> 00:25:24.617
but this is the moment to really be asking

00:25:24.617 --> 00:25:29.324
and trying to answer those
questions in new ways.

00:25:29.324 --> 00:25:32.304
- Yeah (mumbles) one more question,

00:25:32.304 --> 00:25:36.594
there was a question from
Facebook Michelle Miralis Capriati

00:25:36.594 --> 00:25:38.464
and I'll extend her question a little bit.

00:25:38.464 --> 00:25:43.294
She says, how do we work to
retain teachers at this time?

00:25:43.294 --> 00:25:45.504
I know you've done a lot
of research in this area.

00:25:45.504 --> 00:25:47.464
There's a mass exodus
of educators right now

00:25:47.464 --> 00:25:49.344
due to COVID related issues,

00:25:49.344 --> 00:25:51.444
and few and fewer entering the field,

00:25:51.444 --> 00:25:53.514
how much of this is on your
radar at the state board?

00:25:53.514 --> 00:25:55.867
And yeah, what's the solution here?

00:25:57.434 --> 00:26:02.434
- Well, we keep educators by valuing them

00:26:03.744 --> 00:26:05.184
and by supporting them.

00:26:05.184 --> 00:26:10.144
And so number one where
schools are open and childcare

00:26:10.144 --> 00:26:13.864
and other aspects of the
systems, they need to be safe.

00:26:13.864 --> 00:26:17.004
We actually do know a lot
about how to keep them safe.

00:26:17.004 --> 00:26:20.304
We know the importance of face coverings,

00:26:20.304 --> 00:26:23.234
physical distancing, testing and tracking,

00:26:23.234 --> 00:26:26.234
keeping kids and teachers in small cohorts

00:26:26.234 --> 00:26:29.124
and in the countries that
have kept schools open safely,

00:26:29.124 --> 00:26:32.334
they've been very diligent
about those things

00:26:32.334 --> 00:26:34.124
and where we are in environments

00:26:34.124 --> 00:26:37.344
that your infection rates
have been brought down,

00:26:37.344 --> 00:26:39.344
the curve has been been sufficiently,

00:26:39.344 --> 00:26:41.684
we need to be sure that
teachers are supported

00:26:41.684 --> 00:26:44.074
in all of those physical ways,

00:26:44.074 --> 00:26:48.244
as well as in all of the
sort of intellectual ways

00:26:48.244 --> 00:26:52.504
to do the work with kids in
ways that can be successful.

00:26:52.504 --> 00:26:54.604
Same thing is true when
we're in distance learning,

00:26:54.604 --> 00:26:58.574
is how to support that
work so that it's doable,

00:26:58.574 --> 00:27:00.184
so people have opportunities to learn,

00:27:00.184 --> 00:27:05.024
so that they're being supported
and they're being respected.

00:27:05.024 --> 00:27:06.144
Of course, in teaching,

00:27:06.144 --> 00:27:11.144
there are also always issues
of adequate training and pay

00:27:11.784 --> 00:27:14.914
because we don't invest in this country

00:27:14.914 --> 00:27:17.774
in the teaching profession in the way that

00:27:17.774 --> 00:27:21.104
many other countries
do on a regular basis.

00:27:21.104 --> 00:27:24.394
So all of those things
have to be on the table.

00:27:24.394 --> 00:27:26.664
And again, I think we're at a moment

00:27:26.664 --> 00:27:31.054
where parents are appreciating educators

00:27:31.054 --> 00:27:32.924
in ways they didn't before perhaps

00:27:32.924 --> 00:27:36.264
because they can see how
challenging the work is

00:27:36.264 --> 00:27:39.414
with their one or two or maybe
three children in their house

00:27:39.414 --> 00:27:41.114
however many they have

00:27:41.114 --> 00:27:44.244
and can imagine what it
might be to do that with 30

00:27:44.244 --> 00:27:46.504
or whatever the number is in a classroom.

00:27:46.504 --> 00:27:51.144
So I hope we'll see a
redoubling of commitment

00:27:51.144 --> 00:27:54.737
to the support of the teaching profession.

00:27:55.834 --> 00:27:57.502
- Yeah, we couldn't agree more.

00:27:57.502 --> 00:28:00.864
Well we're pretty close to
time, maybe in that last minute,

00:28:00.864 --> 00:28:03.344
if any final thoughts,
and I am also curious,

00:28:03.344 --> 00:28:04.984
how is the state board viewing this?

00:28:04.984 --> 00:28:07.804
Are y'all viewing this
as a one year situation?

00:28:07.804 --> 00:28:11.644
It could it be longer, folks I
know who know about epidemics

00:28:11.644 --> 00:28:14.064
are saying we should at
least plan for longer.

00:28:14.064 --> 00:28:17.514
Is that kind of part of the
conversation at the state level?

00:28:17.514 --> 00:28:19.594
- Well, I think that
there is a recognition

00:28:19.594 --> 00:28:23.167
that we may be in and out of
physical school right now.

00:28:24.920 --> 00:28:26.564
The California government,

00:28:26.564 --> 00:28:28.844
the Department of Public
Health, et cetera,

00:28:28.844 --> 00:28:30.644
getting ready to issue guidance about

00:28:30.644 --> 00:28:33.404
how small groups can come together safely

00:28:33.404 --> 00:28:38.334
even when whole schools are
not able to be actualized

00:28:38.334 --> 00:28:39.984
or in gear.

00:28:39.984 --> 00:28:41.554
But we may have points in time

00:28:41.554 --> 00:28:43.364
and we will in different counties,

00:28:43.364 --> 00:28:46.144
where it is safe to go back to school.

00:28:46.144 --> 00:28:47.774
But then there'll be kids

00:28:47.774 --> 00:28:49.684
who need to be quarantined or something

00:28:49.684 --> 00:28:50.974
and they'll have to be
on distance learning.

00:28:50.974 --> 00:28:53.334
So we're gonna have to
have this flexibility

00:28:53.334 --> 00:28:57.004
in the ways in which a
school is experienced

00:28:57.004 --> 00:28:58.924
in the purpose of education,

00:28:58.924 --> 00:29:02.294
even if the place of education changes.

00:29:02.294 --> 00:29:04.484
And that's why it's so very important

00:29:04.484 --> 00:29:06.254
to close the digital divide,

00:29:06.254 --> 00:29:11.254
help everyone be actively
supported in that work

00:29:11.554 --> 00:29:14.814
because it's going to be a
reality for quite a while.

00:29:14.814 --> 00:29:16.814
And even when get a vaccine,

00:29:16.814 --> 00:29:21.114
which people hope for within this year,

00:29:21.114 --> 00:29:26.114
which will allow us I think to
get back to school in person,

00:29:27.034 --> 00:29:30.274
we're gonna have fires
and floods and hurricanes

00:29:30.274 --> 00:29:34.384
and other climate related
events that are going to make

00:29:34.384 --> 00:29:38.604
the physical place of
schooling a changeable place.

00:29:38.604 --> 00:29:42.174
And we will need to be
prepared for that to be

00:29:42.174 --> 00:29:44.644
the way in which we understand

00:29:44.644 --> 00:29:49.175
that people have to be prepared
to learn wherever they are

00:29:49.175 --> 00:29:52.637
and that we have to support
that in a variety of ways.

00:29:53.984 --> 00:29:56.014
- Now, great, super important message.

00:29:56.014 --> 00:29:57.364
Well, Linda, thank you so much

00:29:57.364 --> 00:29:59.514
and I look forward to
continuing this conversation.

00:29:59.514 --> 00:30:01.434
Hopefully we get you
again on this live stream

00:30:01.434 --> 00:30:02.267
and I actually have

00:30:02.267 --> 00:30:03.974
a lot of stuff I would love
to keep talking to you about,

00:30:03.974 --> 00:30:05.184
of how we could partner together

00:30:05.184 --> 00:30:06.724
to help teachers and families

00:30:06.724 --> 00:30:08.934
around not just California,
but the country.

00:30:08.934 --> 00:30:10.914
Thank you so much, Linda

00:30:10.914 --> 00:30:12.767
- Pleasure to be here, thanks.

00:30:14.904 --> 00:30:17.144
- So thanks everyone again
for joining this live stream.

00:30:17.144 --> 00:30:18.674
Hopefully you found this conversation

00:30:18.674 --> 00:30:19.604
as interesting as I did.

00:30:19.604 --> 00:30:22.044
I always comment about
how fast the time goes.

00:30:22.044 --> 00:30:24.244
There's a many questions my apologies,

00:30:24.244 --> 00:30:26.534
that I wasn't able to get to.

00:30:26.534 --> 00:30:27.684
I will make an announcement.

00:30:27.684 --> 00:30:31.724
We have another leader in
education next Tuesday, John King,

00:30:31.724 --> 00:30:35.634
former US Secretary of Education,
is going to be joining us.

00:30:35.634 --> 00:30:37.484
And as you can imagine,
we're gonna be talking about

00:30:37.484 --> 00:30:39.204
a lot of the the same issues

00:30:39.204 --> 00:30:41.014
about how do we do
distance learning right?

00:30:41.014 --> 00:30:42.784
What are gonna be the implications?

00:30:42.784 --> 00:30:44.924
Are there going to be some silver linings?

00:30:44.924 --> 00:30:46.984
How do we navigate all of this together?

00:30:46.984 --> 00:30:51.984
So I look forward for y'all
to join me on Tuesday.

00:30:52.204 --> 00:30:54.404
So with that stay safe

00:30:54.404 --> 00:30:56.567
and I will see you in
the future live stream.

